0561 ### Reading a snapshot verdict
0562 
0563 Pass the versioned result of `api.check` to `api.snapshot_verdict(report)`. It returns `status`, `exit_code`, `constraints`, `derivations`, `warnings` and `diagnostics`. It accepts plain check schemas 7, 9, 10, 11, 13 and 15–17 and reference wrappers 12 and 14. Unknown versions return `unsupported-schema`. A malformed or unrelated report returns `invalid-report`. A missing population returns `invalid-input` even if all counts are zero. Other statuses are `accepted`, `violated`, `inadequate`, `vacuous`, `unchecked` and `internal-error`. Inspect individual verdicts for mixed outcomes. The accessor preserves the original report. Customer mode exposes only the closed verdict labels and run status, since the supplied report may have been produced under a different policy.
0564 
0565 ```python
0566 from gnd import api
0567 
0568 report = api.check("model.orm.yaml", "facts.json", allow_vacuous=True)
0569 decision = api.snapshot_verdict(report)
0570 ```
0571 
0572 The Python keyword matches `gnd check model.orm.yaml facts.json --allow-vacuous`. It defaults to false, applies to plain snapshots and changes only the acceptance of an otherwise vacuous run. Each vacuous verdict stays visible. For “every requested item has an outcome”, bind the item in a `requested` antecedent and require a matching `outcome` in a guarded existential. With a known request, a covered empty outcome relation violates the rule. Missing outcome coverage leaves it unchecked. A role-local mandatory constraint on outcomes alone can be vacuous for an empty outcome extension.
0573 
0574 The vacuity override uses the CLI's plain schema-7 model policy. Reference-bound, temporal and declared-redaction models are refused by that policy. Anchored, admission, composed and compiled checks cannot be combined with the override.
0575 
0576 ### Reading inferred rows from the check that computed them
0577 
0578 Use `api.check_with_extensions(model, population, fact_types=["active"], max_rows=1000)`. The `report` field contains the ordinary check result, including findings about asserted claims. The `extensions` field contains one result per requested identifier, in request order. A missing claim can be unchecked while its inferred rows are complete.
0579 
0580 A complete relation includes `rows`, which may be empty. Unknown, non-derived, incompletely covered and uncomputable relations return `unavailable`. Declared protected roles or inputs return `protected` under the ordinary applied disclosure policy. This decision precedes the row cap. Each request accepts 1–64 distinct identifiers and a total output budget of 0–10,000 rows. A complete relation that would overflow the remaining budget returns `output-limit` and consumes zero rows. Source and rule evaluation use the checker's ordinary work limits.
0581 
0582 For a semiderived head, complete describes the inferred extension alone. Caller-asserted rows remain in the input snapshot.
0583 
0584 ### Proofs using grounded absence
0585 
0586 A finite witness can use negative premises over covered asserted inputs. Each negative role is grounded by a positive premise or an admitted literal. The proof records the rule, clause, positive premises and exact bindings. Replay reconstructs the entire correlated negative tuple against the bound snapshot. Uncovered and opaque inputs cannot establish absence. An edit invalidates the snapshot binding. Use `why_not` with ordered targets to inspect related blocked tuples against the same loaded inputs.
0587 
0588 ### Persistent local evaluator
0589 
0590 Run `python -m gnd.snapshot_worker` in the evaluator environment. Send one UTF-8 JSON object followed by a newline on stdin. Read one JSON response per line on stdout. A version-1 request contains exactly these fields:
0591 
0592 ```json
0593 {"version":1,"id":"check-1","model":"inventory.orm.yaml","population":{"population":{"model":"inventory.orm.yaml","source":"host","covers":["listed"]},"facts":{"listed":[]}}}
0594 ```
0595 
0596 The model path is local to the worker. Local path aliases resolve to the same canonical file before the bounded read. The caller supplies the population as JSON data. The response echoes the supplied request ID and returns `status="evaluated"`, `model_sha256`, `model_reused` and the ordinary versioned check report. An evaluated population can violate constraints. Interpret its report with `snapshot_verdict`. A protocol or input failure returns `status="error"` with a stable error code. An unusable request ID is returned as null. Maximum input is 4 MiB per physical line, including its newline, and IDs contain 1–128 characters.
0597 
0598 The worker rereads model bytes for each request, reuses one loaded standalone model and EvaluationSession, and replaces both when the model path or digest changes. Reference-population and include-dependent models require their bound inputs and are outside protocol v1. Closing stdin exits the worker. After a process failure, launch a new worker and resend the entire request. Its first valid request loads the model. Use one writer per worker and the ordinary disclosure environment for the intended audience.
